\title{Recurrence, Faithful Observation, and Incidence\\in One-Dimensional Cognitive Dynamics}
\author{Aristotle}
\date{26 July 2026}

\begin{document}
\maketitle

\begin{abstract}
We examine a model in which a cognitive state evolves under a deterministic transition map and a déjà-vu-like event is represented by positive recurrence. For $f:S\to S$, define $P(f)=\{s:\exists n>0,\ f^n(s)=s\}$. Semiconjugacies transport periodic states, while injective semiconjugacies preserve and reflect them. Every continuous self-map of a nondegenerate compact real interval has a periodic state because it has a fixed point. Continuity does not imply density of periodic states: a constant map has precisely one. A constant observation can also report an unchanging value along a nonperiodic hidden trajectory. As an exact case study, $L(x)=(383/100)x(1-x)$ preserves $[0,1]$ and has exactly the fixed points $0$ and $283/383$. These facts do not determine an empirical incidence rate. A defensible incidence model additionally requires a probability space, observation rule, resolution, and time horizon.
\end{abstract}

\section{Introduction}

Déjà vu suggests a return: the present is experienced as if it coincided with something already encountered. A natural abstraction treats cognition as a dynamical system. A state space records possible cognitive configurations, a transition map advances the system, and recurrence occurs when a state returns after finitely many transitions.

The abstraction is diagnostic rather than literal. It separates five questions: Must some state recur? Are recurrent states dense or probabilistically common? Does recurrence survive a change of representation? Does a repeated observation imply a repeated hidden state? Can a dynamical property predict a population percentage?

The answers are distinct. Continuous interval dynamics force at least one fixed point but not dense periodic points. Recurrence passes forward through any dynamics-respecting encoding and backward through an injective one. Lossy observations can manufacture apparent recurrence. Topology alone supplies no probability and hence no lifetime incidence.

\section{Framework}

Let $S$ be a nonempty set and $f:S\to S$. Define $f^0=\operatorname{id}_S$ and $f^{n+1}=f\circ f^n$.

\begin{definition}[Periodic state]
A state $s\in S$ is periodic if there is an integer $n>0$ such that $f^n(s)=s$. Its periodic set is
\[
P(f)=\{s\in S:\exists n\in\mathbb N,\ n>0\text{ and }f^n(s)=s\}.
\]
A state with $f(s)=s$ is a fixed point.
\end{definition}

When $S$ is topological, $A\subseteq S$ is dense if $\overline A=S$. Density is an approximation property, not a probability: dense sets may have measure zero.

\begin{definition}[Semiconjugacy]
For systems $f:S\to S$ and $g:T\to T$, a map $h:S\to T$ is a semiconjugacy when
\[
h\circ f=g\circ h.
\]
It is called faithful here if it is injective.
\end{definition}

An observation $q:S\to O$ assigns an observable value to a hidden state. If $q$ is not injective, equality of observations need not imply equality of hidden states.

\section{Recurrence under representation}

\begin{theorem}[Recurrence Transport]
If $h\circ f=g\circ h$, then $h(P(f))\subseteq P(g)$.
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}
Induction gives $h(f^n(s))=g^n(h(s))$ for every $n\ge0$. If $f^n(s)=s$ for some $n>0$, then
\[
g^n(h(s))=h(f^n(s))=h(s),
\]
so $h(s)$ is periodic.
\end{proof}

\begin{theorem}[Faithful Encoding Characterization]
If $h\circ f=g\circ h$ and $h$ is injective, then
\[
h^{-1}(P(g))=P(f).
\]
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}
The forward transport is the preceding theorem. Conversely, if $g^n(h(s))=h(s)$, the iterated intertwining identity gives $h(f^n(s))=h(s)$. Injectivity implies $f^n(s)=s$.
\end{proof}

\begin{corollary}[Coordinate invariance]
A bijective dynamics-respecting change of coordinates preserves and reflects every positive return.
\end{corollary}

These statements concern positive recurrence. A further refinement can preserve the least positive return time: an earlier return of the encoded point would, by injectivity, force an earlier return of the original point.

\section{Existence without abundance}

\begin{theorem}[Interval Recurrence]
Every continuous self-map $f:[a,b]\to[a,b]$, where $a<b$, has a periodic state. In fact, it has a fixed point.
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}
Set $F(x)=f(x)-x$. Since $f(a),f(b)\in[a,b]$, one has $F(a)\ge0$ and $F(b)\le0$. Continuity and the intermediate value theorem yield $s\in[a,b]$ with $F(s)=0$. Hence $f(s)=s$.
\end{proof}

The theorem says neither that every trajectory returns nor that periodic states are abundant.

\begin{lemma}[Constant Dynamics]
For $c\in\mathbb R$ and $f_c(x)=c$, one has $P(f_c)=\{c\}$.
\end{lemma}

\begin{proof}
The point $c$ is fixed. For every $x$ and $n\ge1$, $f_c^n(x)=c$; therefore a positive return requires $x=c$.
\end{proof}

\begin{theorem}[Continuity Does Not Imply Dense Recurrence]
There is a continuous map $f:\mathbb R\to\mathbb R$ whose periodic set is not dense.
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}
Take $f(x)=0$. The lemma gives $P(f)=\{0\}$, whose closure is not $\mathbb R$.
\end{proof}

Thus
\[
\text{continuous interval self-map}\Longrightarrow P(f)\ne\varnothing,
\]
but continuity alone does not imply $\overline{P(f)}=[a,b]$. Stronger chaotic structure may make periodic points dense on a particular invariant subsystem without making them dense in the whole interval.

\section{Lossy observation}

\begin{theorem}[Constant-Observation False Positive]
Let $f:S\to S$, let $s\notin P(f)$, and let $q:S\to O$ be constant with value $o$. Then for every $n\ge0$,
\[
q(f^n(s))=o=q(s),
\]
although the hidden state $s$ is nonperiodic.
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}
Both observations equal $o$ by the definition of $q$; this is independent of the hidden trajectory.
\end{proof}

Any finite-resolution observation partitions hidden states into observational classes. A repeated report can therefore represent exact recurrence, return to one observational class, or simple insensitivity. With a metric $d_O$ and resolution $\varepsilon>0$, an empirical event might instead be
\[
d_O(q(f^n(s)),q(s))\le\varepsilon.
\]
This is not exact periodicity and depends on the observation protocol.

\section{The logistic map at parameter $3.83$}

Consider
\[
L(x)=\frac{383}{100}x(1-x).
\]

\begin{theorem}[Unit-Interval Invariance]
If $x\in[0,1]$, then $L(x)\in[0,1]$.
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}
The factors $x$ and $1-x$ are nonnegative, so $L(x)\ge0$. Moreover,
\[
x(1-x)=\frac14-\left(x-\frac12\right)^2\le\frac14,
\]
and therefore $L(x)\le383/400<1$.
\end{proof}

\begin{theorem}[Fixed Points at $r=3.83$]
For real $x$, $L(x)=x$ if and only if
\[
x=0\quad\text{or}\quad x=\frac{283}{383}.
\]
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}
The fixed-point equation is equivalent to
\[
383x(1-x)=100x,
\]
then to $x(383x-283)=0$. The zero-product property gives the two values, and substitution verifies both.
\end{proof}

The second fixed point is approximately $0.7389$. This classification is exact and complete for period one. Numerical trajectories at this parameter may suggest a three-cycle, but floating-point proximity does not certify $L^3(x)=x$, exclude smaller periods, or prove attraction.

A rigorous period-three program would isolate three roots of $L^3(x)-x$ in disjoint rational intervals; exclude the fixed points above; verify cyclic mapping of the intervals; and bound
\[
|(L^3)'(x)|=|L'(x)L'(L(x))L'(L^2(x))|
\]
below one on those intervals. The invariant domain and fixed-point classification are prerequisites, not a substitute, for that certification.

\section{Algorithms and numerical interpretation}

Orbit iteration computes $x_{k+1}=rx_k(1-x_k)$ for $0\le k<N$. It uses $O(N)$ time and either $O(1)$ streaming storage or $O(N)$ storage for the full orbit. At $r=3.83$, exact real trajectories beginning in $[0,1]$ stay there.

A tolerance-based detector declares a near return at lag $p$ if
\[
|x_i-x_{i-p}|\le\varepsilon.
\]
Scanning $P$ lags through $N$ observations takes $O(NP)$ time. It detects proximity under a chosen tolerance, not exact periodicity.

Rational arithmetic can evaluate the two fixed-point residuals exactly. Observation experiments can compare an informative quantizer with a constant channel: the latter produces an unchanging symbol even along a changing trajectory, directly illustrating the false-positive theorem.

\section{Topology is not incidence}

A population incidence claim requires a probability space $(\Omega,\mathcal F,\mathbb P)$, subject-dependent dynamics $f_\omega$, initial states, observation maps $q_\omega$, a resolution, and a time horizon $T$. One possible finite-resolution event is
\[
E_{T,\varepsilon}=\left\{\omega:\exists 0\le i<j\le T,\ d_O(q_\omega(s_i),q_\omega(s_j))\le\varepsilon\right\},
\]
where $s_{k+1}=f_\omega(s_k)$. Its modeled incidence is $\mathbb P(E_{T,\varepsilon})$.

Topological density of $P(f)$, natural density in a discretization, invariant-measure weight, finite-time near-return probability, and lifetime population incidence are inequivalent. Exact periodic points can be dense yet have probability zero under a continuous distribution. Coarse observations can create high near-return probability when sampled hidden states are not periodic. The two fixed points of $L$ therefore assign no population percentage.

\section{Discussion and future work}

Within a continuous closed-interval model, a fixed point is unavoidable. This does not show that a generic trajectory reaches it, that a complete cognitive state repeats, or that recurrence produces familiarity. Exact period three, if established, would motivate interval coverings, symbolic itineraries, and scrambled trajectories, but would still not imply an incidence percentage.

Five next steps are natural. First, certify an attracting exact three-cycle at $r=3.83$ by rational root isolation and derivative bounds. Second, strengthen faithful-encoding invariance to minimal periods and the topology of exact-period strata. Third, construct a measurable incidence model with explicit subject and observation distributions. Fourth, derive a symbolic invariant subsystem from an ordered three-cycle. Fifth, establish density of periodic points on a horseshoe's invariant set without overextending the claim to the ambient interval.

Real cognitive systems may involve noise, inputs, and nonstationarity. Stochastic extensions should replace deterministic periodicity by return events and their probabilities. The deterministic framework remains a baseline that exposes which conclusions follow from dynamics alone and which require observation and measure.

\section{Conclusion}

State recurrence, observed recurrence, and incidence are different mathematical objects. Semiconjugacies transport recurrence, and injective semiconjugacies preserve and reflect it. Continuous self-maps of nondegenerate closed intervals have fixed points, but continuity does not imply dense periodic states. Constant observations can create false recurrence. At $r=3.83$, the logistic map preserves $[0,1]$ and has exactly the fixed points $0$ and $283/383$.

Recurrence existence is therefore robust in continuous interval dynamics, while prevalence is not a topological consequence and perception is not transparent access to hidden state. A predictive model must combine a transition law with a specified observation mechanism and probability measure.

\end{document}

